Skip the ArmCpu probe, publish the CPU count here

The photograph of run 587 says where the boot stops.  The panel reads

    1215  nio: C ArmCpu
    1216  P1

and no "nio: P" line follows it.  P1 is written immediately before a
driver's probe() is called; "nio: P" is written immediately after it
returns.  Eight rounds in a row (580..587) stop at the same place, so
the boot thread -- which runs the matching -- is inside that probe and
never comes out.

The same photograph says two earlier fixes worked.  "S1" is on the
panel, which means startCandidate() was reached; before the IOBSD
publication was moved ahead of the matching, the boot stopped before
that mark in a two-second resource wait.  And "D3 D4" follows the
AppleARMCPU candidate, which means its probe returned a driver.

The userland does not need ArmCpu.  The root filesystem this board
boots is the mockfs image in RAM, and the CPU count the kernel needs is
already in the machine topology: ml_parse_cpu_topology() has read the
eight CPUs out of /cpus before this point.  So the probe is not called,
and the count is published directly instead of waiting for a driver to
publish it.

That second change closes the gate the board recorded at run 564.
The boot thread got past the matching then and stopped at w8m3, inside
kperf_init_early(), whose first act is ml_wait_max_cpus().  That call
sleeps until some driver calls ml_set_max_cpus().  On Apple hardware it
is AppleARMSMP, which is not in this kernel; here it is
IOCPUInterruptController::initCPUInterruptController(), reached only
from ArmCpu::start() -- the driver whose probe we have just skipped.
Waiting for it would be waiting for a count nobody can publish.

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
index b60f2ec..3784969 100644
--- a/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
@@ -4652,6 +4652,22 @@ IOService::probeCandidates( OSOrderedSet * matches )
 #endif
 				nio_u_mark("\nP1");
 				nio_match_waypoint = 4;
+				/* nio: this board's ArmCpu driver never returns from probe():
+				 * its start() waits on the GIC/CPU topology that the loader does
+				 * not provide, and the boot thread -- which runs the matching --
+				 * parks here forever (photographs 580..587 all stop at "P1" with
+				 * no "nio: P" after it).  The userland does not need it: the root
+				 * is the mockfs RAMDisk and the CPU count is published in
+				 * startup.c below.  Do not call it. */
+				if (symbol && !strcmp(symbol->getCStringNoCopy(), "ArmCpu")) {
+					nio_u_mark("\nSKIP ArmCpu probe");
+					newInst = NULL;
+					nio_match_waypoint = 5;
+					inst->detach( this );
+					nio_u_mark("\nD1");
+					nio_u_mark("\nD2");
+					continue;
+				}
 				newInst = inst->probe( this, &score );
 				nio_match_waypoint = 5;
 				nio_u_mark("\nnio: P ");
diff --git a/osfmk/kern/startup.c b/osfmk/kern/startup.c
index d7e06a9..5c4ff34 100644
--- a/osfmk/kern/startup.c
+++ b/osfmk/kern/startup.c
@@ -1101,6 +1101,21 @@ kernel_bootstrap_thread(void)
 	vm_set_restrictions(machine_info.max_cpus);
 	nio_boot_waypoint = 8;
 
+	/* nio: publish the CPU count here rather than waiting for IOKit.
+	 *
+	 * ml_wait_max_cpus() (the first thing kperf_init_early() does) sleeps
+	 * until some driver calls ml_set_max_cpus().  On Apple hardware that is
+	 * AppleARMSMP, which is not in this kernel.  Here it is
+	 * IOCPUInterruptController::initCPUInterruptController(), reached only
+	 * from ArmCpu::start() -- the driver whose probe we just skipped because
+	 * it never returns.  Otherwise the boot thread waits for a count nobody
+	 * can publish, exactly as run 564 showed (w8m3).
+	 *
+	 * The topology is parsed from /cpus by now: 8 CPUs, one "running".
+	 * Publish that count. */
+	extern void ml_set_max_cpus(unsigned int);
+	ml_set_max_cpus((unsigned int)(ml_get_max_cpu_number() + 1));
+	nio_dc_puts("\nnio: CPU count published");
 
 #if KPERF
 	kperf_init_early();
